course: dictionary
lesson: 9956
title: Tattling and telling
level: primary-5
stars: 1
explain: «Tattling» means telling an adult about someone's small misbehaviour, often just to get them into trouble. This meaning is mainly used in American English. Tattling can also mean gossiping or chattering.
explain: Context helps you tell them apart: «Telling is not tattling: tattling is trying to get someone into trouble, while telling is trying to get someone out of trouble or keep them safe.» The reason behind the words shows which one it is.
explain: Ask yourself: is this to keep someone safe, or to get someone into trouble? If someone is hurt, in danger, or being bullied, always tell an adult.
fact: Tattling means telling on someone's small misbehaviour to get them into trouble.
fact: Tattling can also mean gossiping or chattering.
fact: Telling means telling an adult to keep someone safe or get them out of trouble.
fact: Always tell an adult if someone is hurt, in danger, or being bullied.
fact: A tattletale is a person who tattles.
exercise: Ruby says, «Stop tattling, Sam.» What is Ruby asking Sam to do? | Stop telling the teacher that someone is hurt | Stop writing his name | Stop singing in class | *Stop telling on a friend just to get them into trouble
exercise: Which one is telling, not tattling? | Telling a joke at lunch | Telling an adult that your friend chewed gum in class, just to get her in trouble | *Telling an adult that a classmate has fallen and is hurt | Telling your mum that your brother's name is silly
exercise: What can tattling also mean? | Running very fast | Sleeping at the desk | *Gossiping or chattering | Drawing pictures
test: Mia sees a friend being bullied at lunch. What should she do? | Post it online for everyone to see | *Tell an adult, because it is telling, not tattling | Stay quiet, because telling is always tattling | Wait and hide the problem
test: Tom says, «You're such a tattletale.» What does tattletale mean? | A kind helper who is always fair | A person who is very good at reading | *A person who tells on others to get them into trouble | A type of bird
exam: Which sentence shows telling, not tattling? | Telling a joke to make your friend laugh | *Telling the teacher that a girl fell off the climbing frame and needs help | Telling the class that Jacob sneezed on purpose just so he gets into trouble | Telling everyone what you ate for breakfast
